WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.960
When a tool breaks, what it sends back matters as much as what it does when it works.

2
00:00:05.599 --> 00:00:11.439
Claude reads that response to decide whether to retry, ask you directly, or try something else.

3
00:00:12.159 --> 00:00:17.040
In this video, we'll design tool errors that carry real information,

4
00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:21.280
and cover why anything a tool returns deserves a measure of suspicion.

5
00:00:22.399 --> 00:00:27.040
A tool that fails and returns the word error has told Claude almost nothing.

6
00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:33.680
A tool error response is a structured reply that says execution failed and explains why,

7
00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:40.319
carrying an error type, a human-readable message, and optional metadata Claude can reason over.

8
00:00:40.319 --> 00:00:43.919
That structure is what turns a dead end into an actionable signal.

9
00:00:44.479 --> 00:00:51.439
Inside it, the error code is a short, stable string that code and models can act on without

10
00:00:51.439 --> 00:00:56.080
reading the message text. The suggested action tells Claude what to do next,

11
00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:02.080
whether that's retry, ask the user, or escalate. Together, these pieces give Claude a complete

12
00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:08.319
picture of what went wrong and how to respond. It helps to know what the tool result block

13
00:01:08.319 --> 00:01:13.760
actually looks like when Claude sends it back, because it's leaner than people expect. It

14
00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:20.800
carries just three fields. The tool use ID matches the result back to the specific call Claude made.

15
00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:26.319
The content field holds the result itself, and when the tool fails, that same field holds your

16
00:01:26.319 --> 00:01:33.120
error message. And the is error flag set to true tells Claude the execution failed rather than

17
00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:38.239
succeeded. There's no dedicated error code field, no separate context field, nothing else in the

18
00:01:38.239 --> 00:01:45.440
block. So any richer error, shape you design, a code, a reason, a suggested action lives inside

19
00:01:45.519 --> 00:01:51.440
the content field as text. Claude reads it there. Keep that in mind. The structure you invent is a

20
00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:57.360
convention inside content, not extra fields on the block itself. One of the most useful

21
00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:02.160
distinctions in error handling is whether the failure is a user error or a system error.

22
00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:08.880
User errors mean the caller sent bad data, the wrong format, a missing required field,

23
00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:15.360
or a value that's out of range. For these, retrying won't help. Claude should surface the

24
00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:21.360
problem to the user and ask for a correction. System errors are different. A timeout, a service

25
00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:27.679
that's temporarily unavailable, or an unexpected upstream response are all things the user didn't

26
00:02:27.679 --> 00:02:34.559
cause and can't fix. For these, Claude can retry, wait, or try an alternative path without involving

27
00:02:34.559 --> 00:02:40.880
the user at all. Your error code should make this distinction explicit. When you write error

28
00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:46.880
messages, keep in mind that Claude is the primary consumer, not a developer reading logs. Codes need

29
00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:52.479
to stay stable across versions, so any routing logic built on them doesn't break when you ship

30
00:02:52.479 --> 00:02:58.080
an update. Include the input value that triggered the error in the context field, because the same

31
00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:04.080
code can mean different things depending on which parameter failed. And avoid raw stack traces as

32
00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:10.479
the main message. A stack trace is useful in a log file, but it's noise in a tool response. Replace

33
00:03:10.479 --> 00:03:16.160
it with a clean message and put the technical details somewhere else. The goal is an error

34
00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:23.039
that Claude can act on, not just read. These four fields form the skeleton of a well-designed error

35
00:03:23.039 --> 00:03:29.440
response. The error code is the stable, machine-readable string that never changes across

36
00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:34.240
versions. The message is the human-readable description you'd be comfortable surfacing

37
00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:40.080
directly in a chat reply without editing. The context block carries the key value pairs that

38
00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:46.960
identify what failed and why, so debugging doesn't require guessing. And the suggested action is the

39
00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:52.320
explicit hint about what Claude should do next, which saves it from having to infer the right path

40
00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:59.279
on its own. Each field serves a different consumer. The code serves routing logic. The message serves

41
00:03:59.279 --> 00:04:06.000
users. The context serves debugging. The suggested action serves Claude's decision engine. Design

42
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:12.479
each one with its consumer in mind. The suggested action field is optional but valuable, because it

43
00:04:12.479 --> 00:04:18.720
points Claude toward either recovery or escalation. Recovery means Claude handles the problem on its

44
00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:24.720
own. It tries again with a different value, reformats its input, or switches to an alternative

45
00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:30.640
tool. Escalation means Claude brings a human into the loop. Because the problem needs judgment,

46
00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:37.760
the model can't provide. A rate-limited error should say to retry after a short delay. An

47
00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:44.640
incorrect input error should say to ask the user to correct the value. A service-unavailable error

48
00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:50.799
might say to try an alternative tool or escalate. Error routing is what ties this together,

49
00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:56.480
using the error code to pick the right path automatically. A well-designed set of codes

50
00:04:56.480 --> 00:05:01.359
lets you define those routing rules once and apply them across every tool you own.

51
00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:08.959
A few mistakes show up repeatedly in tool error design. The first is returning a success status

52
00:05:08.959 --> 00:05:14.320
with an error buried somewhere in the response body. Claude may not look for it, and the model

53
00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:20.480
will proceed as if the call succeeded. The second is using vague codes like failed or error with no

54
00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:26.320
subtype, which forces Claude to parse the message to understand what happened. The third is leaving

55
00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:32.079
out context, when the same code can mean different things depending on which input failed. Each of

56
00:05:32.079 --> 00:05:36.959
these mistakes adds friction to recovery and increases the chance that Claude makes the wrong

57
00:05:36.959 --> 00:05:44.160
next move. Specific, context-rich errors are worth the extra effort. There's one more thing to

58
00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:50.160
internalize, and it's a security point, not a formatting one. A tool result often carries content

59
00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:56.720
from sources you don't control. A fetched web page, an inbound email, a user upload, a third-party

60
00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:03.679
service response. You have to treat all of that as untrusted input. An attacker who can influence it

61
00:06:03.679 --> 00:06:10.000
may bury instructions in the text that try to redirect Claude, telling it to ignore its task

62
00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:16.880
or leak data. That's indirect prompt injection, and it turns your tool into a confused deputy

63
00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:22.880
acting on someone else's orders. The guardrail Anthropic recommends is to keep that untrusted

64
00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:29.760
content inside tool result blocks. Never promote it into the system prompt or a plain user message,

65
00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:34.079
so the model always knows it came from a tool and treats it with appropriate suspicion.

66
00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:41.040
Here's the big picture. A useful tool error combines a stable error code, a clear message,

67
00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:47.040
enough context to know what failed, and an explicit suggested action. Distinguish user

68
00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:54.160
errors from system errors so Claude retries only when retrying can actually help, and treat whatever

69
00:06:54.160 --> 00:07:00.640
a tool hands back as untrusted content. Get those pieces right and Claude recovers or escalates

70
00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:04.880
without guessing. That wraps up this one. I'll see you in the next lesson.

